 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518090 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518090 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518090 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518090 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518090 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518090 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8000223-fc08-5e87-86f4-e925b30d30c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518099 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2d/2dd50d4970f5c738c7cc350b8aee4668a6ec9ce4b1444698c5ecd737d01f82ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01274ce5-42e5-5d91-b593-1351d7ffb44f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518099 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-2$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9eae519cd7ca99aa85fe62c9fa8276a7455cbc869587296405c6a6b3caf2ee30.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-2,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=-2.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/1a/1a2fd6311c5845885bfd7ed9118091a5c81c4fea8c81587db79b203a51b9e1de.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/f6/f6e473044a84d215de98e3739bea83ccaf107231ccb273c8e45db91221f05edb.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81019" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518102 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2a/2ab398982e30d4b0ce633b77541596f3f5848e1b0a25539037ec3ee0fc557d4d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02de7b14-03bb-5d7e-b753-b48cb91786a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518102 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1.$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\n \n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical line $x=-1$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/77/7719107149a10c6e5a22e6c343b532f3580582ebd78d3667aad5f2684d2f219d.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,0)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=0$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is constant and positive  to the right of $x=0$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing straight line on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/d8/d81008f3d06f41f53a389fa2f36a7b12845fa3dae5a2707a739bbb500c8b4e56.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/76/763163652b41a7b3cc0b7a6b3faca4799cc073238a50afd9bd3d9c7767c32793.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518104 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/28a16cda2f97557c23908fcd45928da5d4e11102343c33eaf07d1990fb0b6baf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06d5e4de-57e7-5985-961d-b5e59bdab913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518104 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=0$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea6ac357860556acba25d5510ff0e65a4b66acc65e035cd218715b0e96807519.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,0)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(0,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/2a/2aafc298a8a7b0856d339582bb1236e9ae97c728eba103c703998e14455fdde5.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/04/04ec8de5abfecc23f3b4fb844b4e725e2a4f94aeb12bef61cda6356a6522826c.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518107 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=|x+5|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bf75373-8a0d-58e6-9585-31096e6c716e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518107 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/ae/ae96c