ssible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/93/938c86a415620a2e80873f7cee72b2ebaf5579ae2e67eb966bb1f8836e62c087.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518179 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518179 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518179 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518179 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|x+1|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518179 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fc26721-45c6-5872-bde4-819ab63691ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518179 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c4e03ef210691e4eee922d0c35e90abb9cde1a78064ad491d4ac084d875c4a57.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40b5e9b8928be63a415877c0633f0302c874db51b1c74a1f82b7b1526a6f4d14.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=-1$ and goes down to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=-1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=-1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/c6/c68544957f33afdeab436a3fbf75b9e27990a2e7f3ea75f39c6e58dce24f6185.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/4c/4c91ba8f06642fe14035400efa61dbd2d8bbadd07274fedc9a90678970bbb9a8.png)\n\n \n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518203 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518203 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518203 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518203 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9e/9e7c8d7f80211aebbd8a8fb5456e2bfe0a5dd0dc46addfe65777eb09f0b4c66a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518203 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d823907c-c12e-50eb-a396-a258624fbd1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518203 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=3$  \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical lines $x=3$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/4a/4aa4592a6edae5207c7e934aa148e87dd8a8762f6bbdba260cc137bd64b407b9.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(2,3)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(3,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=3.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is a positive constant to the left of $x=2$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing  straight line on $(-\\infty ,2).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the right of $x=2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/34/347dda820c8307984b88ffb5f2b528c20984ec8a7e9acbe9b7f3d8f457a917b2.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/99/99de5224b614ea11b454020d7a165b58ff0fcf47708faba9973255276133eebc.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518212 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518212 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518212 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518212 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a5/a53074660204fdd4fd62575ef8db80a3b04144ef7b9e18d98c60fbbe9a480ee3.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518212 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e89c21de-ead1-56e3-b348-ff522b66f5d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518212 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=1$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/12/12e6a8036759b8bbcc2f4fbe4ae7f4f7e18278de65fbdc0faa99c919f7234284.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](images/38/38f1c92810a3311ed939a54ac2856cb7ada111dbf24528785f80bd5f9ca49c84.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/79/7921a107b33a9b9bdc3df8dec418828f4cd5de2d89a9e7ab41845b0c64bbb653.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
